 "standard_text": "A.11A simplify numerical radical expressions involving square roots; A.11B simplify numeric and algebraic expressions using the laws of exponents, including integral and rational exponents",
 "meta_desc": "Free Algebra I worksheets for Texas TEKS A.11A and A.11B: simplify square roots, add and multiply radicals, laws of exponents and rational exponents. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Take out perfect squares; add exponents when multiplying.",
 "key_sub": "√75 = 5√3 and x² · x³ = x⁵.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>√72 = √36 × √2 = 6√2: take out the largest perfect square.</li><li><i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup> · <i>x</i><sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i>+<i>b</i></sup>; (<i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup>)<sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>ab</i></sup>; <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i></sup> ÷ <i>x</i><sup><i>b</i></sup> = <i>x</i><sup><i>a</i>−<i>b</i></sup>.</li><li><i>x</i><sup>0</sup> = 1 and <i>x</i><sup>−<i>n</i></sup> = 1 ÷ <i>x</i><sup><i>n</i></sup>.</li><li><i>x</i><sup>1/2</sup> = √<i>x</i>.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>√75</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>√25 × √3</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 5√3</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rooting only part of the number</td><td>√72 is 6√2, not 36√2.</td></tr><tr><td>Multiplying exponents when multiplying powers</td><td><i>x</i><sup>2</sup> · <i>x</i><sup>3</sup> = <i>x</i><sup>5</sup>.</td></tr><tr><td>Reading <i>x</i><sup>−2</sup> as −<i>x</i><sup>2</sup></td><td>It is 1 ÷ <i>x</i><sup>2</sup>.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
